::ALG2_U05_L02_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Boundary for y &lt; x + 4? {
= Dashed#Correct. Strict inequality excludes the boundary.
~ Solid#Solid includes equality.
~ Vertical only#The boundary is y=x+4.
~ No boundary#A boundary is needed.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Boundary for y &le; 3x - 2? {
~ Dashed#Dashed is for strict inequalities.
= Solid#Correct. The symbol &le; includes equality.
~ No boundary#A boundary line is still graphed.
~ Only x-axis#The boundary is y=3x-2.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Boundary for y &gt; -2x + 5? {
~ Solid#Solid includes equality.
~ No boundary#Graph the related line.
= Dashed#Correct. The symbol &gt; is strict.
~ Only y-axis#The boundary is not the y-axis.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Boundary for y &ge; 4? {
~ Dashed horizontal line#Dashed would exclude equality.
~ Solid vertical line#y=4 is horizontal.
~ No boundary#The boundary is needed.
= Solid horizontal line#Correct. The boundary is y=4 and equality is included.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y &ge; x + 1? {
= (2,3)#Correct. 3 &ge; 3 is true.
~ (2,2)#2 &ge; 3 is false.
~ (0,0)#0 &ge; 1 is false.
~ (-1,-2)#-2 &ge; 0 is false.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y &lt; -x + 3? {
~ (1,2)#2 &lt; 2 is false because equality is excluded.
= (1,1)#Correct. 1 &lt; 2 is true.
~ (0,4)#4 &lt; 3 is false.
~ (-1,5)#5 &lt; 4 is false.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies both y &le; x + 2 and y &ge; 0? {
~ (1,5)#5 &le; 3 is false.
~ (0,-1)#-1 &ge; 0 is false.
= (1,2)#Correct. 2 &le; 3 and 2 &ge; 0 are both true.
~ (-2,2)#2 &le; 0 is false.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point is feasible for x &ge; 0 and y &ge; 0? {
~ (-2,3)#x is negative.
~ (2,-3)#y is negative.
~ (-2,-3)#Both coordinates are negative.
= (2,3)#Correct. Both coordinates are nonnegative.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>For y &le; -x + 4, which side is shaded? {
= Below the line#Correct. Less than or equal means y-values at or below the boundary.
~ Above the line#Above matches greater than.
~ Only on the line#The line is included, but the region below is also included.
~ No side#A half-plane is shaded.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>For y &gt; 2x - 3, which side is shaded? {
~ Below the line#Below is less than.
= Above the line#Correct. Greater than means above.
~ Only on the line#The line is not included.
~ Both sides#Only one side is shaded.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>What does a constraint represent in a context? {
~ Only the final answer#A constraint defines possible answers.
~ A point that always fails#Constraints can be satisfied.
= A condition or limit that solutions must satisfy#Correct. Constraints restrict feasible choices.
~ A graph with no meaning#Constraints have contextual meaning.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the feasible region of a system? {
~ The union of all shaded regions#Union would include points that satisfy only one inequality.
~ Only the steepest line#Lines are boundaries, not full feasible regions.
~ Only the origin#The origin may or may not be feasible.
= The overlap where all inequalities are true#Correct. Every inequality must be satisfied.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which symbol uses a dashed boundary? {
= &lt;#Correct. Strict inequalities use dashed boundaries.
~ &le;#This uses solid.
~ &ge;#This uses solid.
~ = #This is just a boundary equation.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which symbol uses a solid boundary? {
~ &gt;#Strict inequality is dashed.
= &ge;#Correct. Equality is included.
~ &lt;#Strict inequality is dashed.
~ &ne;#This is not a standard half-plane boundary symbol.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>A point in the overlap of two shaded regions means what? {
~ It satisfies neither inequality#That would be outside both.
~ It satisfies only one inequality#That would be outside the overlap.
= It satisfies both inequalities#Correct. The overlap is where all constraints are true.
~ It is always on both boundaries#Interior feasible points do not have to lie on boundaries.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which is true for y &lt; 5? {
~ Solid horizontal boundary and shade below#Strict inequality should be dashed.
~ Dashed vertical boundary#y=5 is horizontal.
~ Shade above#Above would be y>5.
= Dashed horizontal boundary and shade below#Correct. y values less than 5 are below y=5 and equality is excluded.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which is true for x &ge; -2? {
= Solid vertical boundary and shade right#Correct. x-values greater than or equal to -2 are to the right of x=-2.
~ Dashed vertical boundary and shade right#Equality is included, so solid.
~ Solid horizontal boundary#x=-2 is vertical.
~ Shade left#Left would be x<=-2.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies x &le; 4 and y &gt; 1? {
~ (5,2)#5 &le; 4 is false.
= (3,2)#Correct. 3 &le; 4 and 2 &gt; 1.
~ (3,1)#1 &gt; 1 is false.
~ (5,1)#Both conditions fail.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Why test a point when graphing an inequality? {
~ To find the slope only#Slope comes from the boundary equation.
~ To remove the boundary#Testing does not remove boundaries.
= To determine which side of the boundary to shade#Correct. A test point shows where the inequality is true.
~ To avoid graphing#The graph is still needed.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>If the feasible region is empty, what does that mean? {
~ Every point is feasible#That is the opposite of empty.
~ Only the origin is feasible#Empty means no points.
~ The boundaries disappear#Boundaries may exist even without overlap.
= No point satisfies all constraints#Correct. The constraints cannot all be true at once.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y &le; 2x and y &gt; x - 1? {
= (2,3)#Correct. 3 &le; 4 and 3 &gt; 1.
~ (1,3)#3 &le; 2 is false.
~ (0,-2)#-2 &gt; -1 is false.
~ (2,5)#5 &le; 4 is false.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>In a production context, x &ge; 0 and y &ge; 0 usually mean what? {
~ Both quantities must be zero#Nonnegative includes positive values.
= Quantities cannot be negative#Correct. Production amounts are nonnegative.
~ Only x matters#Both constraints matter.
~ The graph has no feasible region#There can be many feasible points.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the first step to graph y &le; x - 3? {
~ Graph a dashed boundary#Dashed is for strict inequalities.
~ Shade before drawing a boundary#The boundary guides the shading.
= Graph the solid boundary y = x - 3#Correct. Use the related equation and include equality.
~ Graph x = -3#The boundary is y=x-3.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point is NOT feasible for y &ge; 0 and x &ge; 0? {
~ (0,0)#Both coordinates are nonnegative.
~ (2,0)#Both coordinates are nonnegative.
~ (3,4)#Both coordinates are positive.
= (-1,2)#Correct. The x-coordinate is negative.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>What does checking a point in every inequality prevent? {
= Accepting a point that satisfies only part of the system#Correct. Systems require all constraints to be true.
~ Changing the inequality symbols#Checking does not change symbols.
~ Drawing all boundaries solid#Boundary style depends on the symbols.
~ Making every point feasible#Point testing identifies true and false points.
}

